I was reading a fascinating piece on context engineering today1 when it hit me: we&rsquo;ve been overthinking AI product manager interviews. Instead of complex case studies or theoretical questions, there&rsquo;s one simple ask that reveals everything: &ldquo;Show me your best prompt.&rdquo;
It&rsquo;s like asking a developer to show their cleanest code. In a single example, you can assess their understanding of AI systems, their practical experience, and their ability to communicate with machines - the core competency of AI product management.
Why This Question Works A prompt isn&rsquo;t just text - it&rsquo;s a window into someone&rsquo;s mental model of AI systems. When someone shares their best prompt, they&rsquo;re revealing:
Their understanding of context hierarchy - How they structure information Their grasp of model limitations - What they account for and avoid Their iteration mindset - Evidence of refinement through testing Their practical experience - Real-world application vs. theoretical knowledge Their communication clarity - How they translate business needs into AI instructions Unlike hypothetical scenarios, prompts are artifacts of actual work. They can&rsquo;t be faked or memorized from interview prep guides.
The Evaluation Framework Here&rsquo;s how to assess what you&rsquo;re seeing:
1. Structure and Clarity (Anthropic Fundamentals) Green Flags:
Clear, direct instructions without unnecessary fluff2 Logical information hierarchy XML tags or markdown for structure (&lt;example&gt;, &lt;context&gt;, &lt;output_format&gt;)3 Explicit success criteria defined upfront Red Flags:
Vague or overly creative language No clear structure or organization Missing context or incomplete instructions Trying to be clever instead of clear 2. Model-Specific Knowledge This is where it gets interesting. Different models require different approaches:
For Traditional Models (GPT-4, Claude):
Uses examples and chain-of-thought reasoning4 Includes role assignment (&ldquo;You are an expert&hellip;&rdquo;) Provides multiple examples to guide behavior Shows understanding of token efficiency For Reasoning Models (OpenAI o1/o3/o4-mini):
Earlier models (o1) avoided chain-of-thought instructions entirely5 Newer models (o3/o4-mini) show improved instruction following6 Direct, concise problem statements still preferred Less reliance on few-shot examples compared to traditional models Focus on clear problem definition over process guidance A candidate who shows both approaches demonstrates they understand the fundamental differences between model architectures.
3. Tool Integration Competency For Function Calling/Code Interpreter:
Clear tool usage boundaries7 Explicit instructions about when to use which tool Argument validation requirements Planning and reflection steps built into the workflow8 Advanced Indicators:
Evidence of A/B testing different approaches Handling of edge cases and error conditions Integration with RAG systems or external APIs Understanding of context length limitations 4. Real-World Readiness Quality Indicators:
Handles ambiguous or incomplete user inputs Includes output format specifications Shows evidence of iteration and refinement Demonstrates understanding of business constraints Experience Markers:
References to performance metrics or success criteria Understanding of cost implications (token usage, latency) Consideration of user experience in AI interactions Integration with existing business processes What to Look For: Green Flags vs Red Flags Green Flags ✅ Specific, testable success criteria - &ldquo;Generate 3 alternative headlines, each under 60 characters&rdquo; Context boundaries - Clear separation of instructions, examples, and user input Error handling - What to do when inputs are unclear or incomplete Output constraints - Format, length, tone specifications Evidence of testing - &ldquo;I found this works better when&hellip;&rdquo; or version comments Red Flags ❌ Generic templates - Obviously copied from online guides Overly complex instructions - Trying to solve everything in one prompt No structure - Wall of text without clear organization Missing context - No explanation of what problem this solves Outdated techniques - Using 2022 prompting advice for 2025 models Follow-Up Questions to Dig Deeper Once you&rsquo;ve seen their best prompt, these questions reveal even more:
&ldquo;Walk me through how you developed this prompt.&rdquo; - Shows their iteration process &ldquo;What didn&rsquo;t work in earlier versions?&rdquo; - Reveals their testing methodology &ldquo;How would you modify this for a different model?&rdquo; - Tests model-specific knowledge &ldquo;What metrics do you use to evaluate prompt performance?&rdquo; - Shows business thinking &ldquo;How do you handle edge cases with this prompt?&rdquo; - Demonstrates real-world experience The Meta-Question Here&rsquo;s the beautiful part: asking this question is itself a form of context engineering. You&rsquo;re:
Clearly defining the task (show me your best work) Providing specific success criteria (reveal AI competency) Creating a structured evaluation framework Testing for practical experience over theoretical knowledge The candidates who recognize this meta-layer - that your interview question is a well-engineered prompt - are the ones who truly understand AI product management.
Beyond the Prompt The best candidates won&rsquo;t just show you a prompt - they&rsquo;ll tell you the story. They&rsquo;ll explain the business problem, the iteration process, the metrics they tracked, and the lessons learned. They&rsquo;ll show you how they think about AI systems as products, not just tools.
Because that&rsquo;s ultimately what AI product management is about: building bridges between human needs and machine capabilities, one well-crafted prompt at a time.
What&rsquo;s your best prompt? I&rsquo;d love to see examples of great prompts that reveal deep AI understanding. Share them with me on X @emjayzo.
